14. Mutual Fund Performance Analysis

The scheme performance dataset contains several important performance indicators.

Return Metrics

The project evaluates:

1-year return
3-year return
5-year return

Longer-term returns are useful for evaluating consistency over different investment horizons.

Benchmark Comparison

The project compares scheme performance against benchmark returns.

The difference between fund return and benchmark performance can be used to identify funds that outperform or underperform their benchmark.

Alpha

Alpha measures the excess performance of a fund relative to its benchmark.

A positive alpha can indicate outperformance relative to the benchmark.

Beta

Beta measures the sensitivity of the fund to market movements.

A higher beta generally indicates greater sensitivity to market movements.

15. Risk Analysis

Risk analysis is an important component of mutual fund evaluation.

The project includes:

Sharpe Ratio

The Sharpe ratio evaluates return relative to risk.

A higher Sharpe ratio generally indicates better risk-adjusted performance.

Sortino Ratio

The Sortino ratio focuses on downside volatility and evaluates return relative to downside risk.

Standard Deviation

Standard deviation measures the volatility of fund returns.

Higher standard deviation indicates greater variability in returns.

Maximum Drawdown

Maximum drawdown measures the largest decline from a peak to a subsequent trough.

It helps understand potential downside experienced by investors.

16. Advanced Analytics

The project includes additional analytical work beyond basic EDA.

Rolling Sharpe Analysis

Rolling Sharpe analysis helps evaluate how risk-adjusted performance changes over time.

The project generated:

reports/rolling_sharpe_chart.png

This analysis helps identify periods when risk-adjusted performance improves or deteriorates.

Sharpe Summary

A summary file was generated:

reports/sharpe_summary.csv

It provides a structured view of Sharpe ratio performance.

VaR and CVaR

The project also generated:

reports/var_cvar_report.csv

Value at Risk (VaR) estimates potential losses under a specified confidence level.

Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR) estimates the expected loss when losses exceed the VaR threshold.

These metrics provide additional insight into downside risk.

